# Line endings are declared HERE, not left to core.autocrlf.
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM config
# (C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig), so it is on for every Windows clone without
# ever appearing in --local or --global — which is why it was so hard to find.
#
# The objects in this repo were always LF. autocrlf=true was converting them to CRLF
# on checkout, so the working tree and the repo disagreed on every text file. That
# mismatch is what produced the constant "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings, files
# reported as modified while being byte-identical, and rebases and merges that failed
# with "local changes would be overwritten" on a clean tree.
#
# text=auto  — git decides text vs binary, and stores text as LF in the objects.
# eol=lf     — and checks it out as LF too, on every platform.
#
# So the working tree now matches the objects and NOTHING is converted in either
# direction. `-c core.autocrlf=false` is no longer needed on rebases; passing it is in
# fact harmful, because it makes git re-read the tree without the conversion the index
# was written under and see phantom modifications.
#
# ⚠ text=auto has a safety valve: it will NOT normalise a file that was committed with
# CRLF, deliberately, to avoid churn. Three files here really were CRLF in the objects
# (two rawdata JSON, the logo SVG) and `git add --renormalize` silently skipped them —
# they had to be rewritten to LF on disk first. If a stubborn file ever refuses to
# normalise, that is why.
#
# Nothing in the toolchain wants CRLF: there are no .bat or .cmd files, and Node,
# ESLint and Foundry all read LF natively on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Foundry compendium packs are LevelDB databases. A few of their internal files
# (CURRENT, LOG, LOG.old, MANIFEST-*) are pure ASCII, so git's text detection treats
# them as text and rewrites their line endings on checkout wherever core.autocrlf is
# enabled — i.e. on most Windows clones.
#
# That is fatal for CURRENT, which must contain exactly "MANIFEST-NNNNNN\n". With a
# CRLF, LevelDB strips only the trailing \n and then looks for a file literally named
# "MANIFEST-NNNNNN\r", which cannot exist — the pack fails to open and the compendium
# is unusable. The .ldb files escape this only because they contain NUL bytes and are
# therefore auto-detected as binary.
#
# Mark the whole pack tree binary so no content filtering is ever applied to it.
packs/** binary

# Kept deliberately broad: if packs are ever shipped from a modules/ tree again,
# they are the same LevelDB format and need identical protection.
modules/**/packs/** binary

# Git hooks are run through `sh`. With core.autocrlf enabled — the default on most
# Windows clones — they would be checked out with CRLF endings, and sh fails on the
# carriage return in the shebang line ("bad interpreter"). Pin them to LF so the
# hooks survive a Windows checkout.
.githooks/** text eol=lf
